import{f as c,J as h,o as d,c as l,a6 as o,b as s,w as t,a as r,K as i,a7 as n,ac as p,ad as g,ae as m,af as f,ag as u,ah as b,ai as y,aj as w,ak as k,al as v,am as S,an as T,ao as E,ap as x,aq as A,ar as C,as as _,at as L,au as P,av as R,aw as I,ax as D}from"./chunks/framework.DuKjrNUc.js";const q=JSON.parse('{"title":"Terminal UI","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"tui.md","filePath":"tui.md"}'),N={name:"tui.md"};function M(O,e,B,F,Q,U){const a=h("Mermaid");return d(),l("div",null,[e[3]||(e[3]=o('<h1 id="terminal-ui" tabindex="-1">Terminal UI <a class="header-anchor" href="#terminal-ui" aria-label="Permalink to “Terminal UI”">​</a></h1><p>Run builds, apply changes, operate SQL pipelines, and explore the database from the keyboard.</p><div class="language-bash"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">noorm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ui</span></span></code></pre></div><p><img src="'+p+'" alt="Adding a config, creating the database, building the schema, fast-forwarding changes, and browsing the result" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>noorm ui</code> takes no flags and opens on Home. Every other <code>noorm</code> command is a non-interactive CLI, and bare <code>noorm</code> prints the command list; see the <a href="/headless.html">CLI reference</a>.</p><h2 id="how-do-i-get-around" tabindex="-1">How do I get around? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-get-around" aria-label="Permalink to “How do I get around?”">​</a></h2><p>Home opens five working screens with one key. Config, changes, run, and database navigation are below; the <a href="#pipelines">pipeline screens</a> have their own review and history path:</p>',7)),(d(),s(n,null,{default:t(()=>[i(a,{id:"mermaid-19",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20%20%20Home%5B%22Home%22%5D%20--%3E%7Cc%7C%20Config%5B%22Config%20list%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Home%20--%3E%7Cg%7C%20Changes%5B%22Changes%20list%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Home%20--%3E%7Cr%7C%20Run%5B%22Run%20menu%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Home%20--%3E%7Cd%7C%20Db%5B%22Database%20menu%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Config%20--%3E%7Ck%7C%20Secrets%5B%22Secrets%20of%20the%3Cbr%2F%3Ehighlighted%20config%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Db%20--%3E%7Cx%7C%20Explore%5B%22Schema%20explorer%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:t(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[4]||(e[4]=o('<p><img src="'+g+'" alt="Home: status, quick actions, and recent activity" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key on Home</th><th>Opens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Run menu</td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>Config list</td></tr><tr><td><code>g</code></td><td>Changes list</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>Database menu</td></tr><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>Pipeline list</td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>More: Settings, Vault, Identity, Lock</td></tr><tr><td><code>s</code>, <code>v</code>, <code>i</code>, <code>l</code></td><td>Settings, Vault, Identity, Lock, without going through More</td></tr><tr><td><code>u</code></td><td>Update</td></tr><tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Run build</td></tr><tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Fast-forward changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>Lock status</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>Quit</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Keys</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Switch config</td><td><code>c</code>, <code>↓</code> to the config, <code>Enter</code></td></tr><tr><td>Fast-forward all changes</td><td><code>g</code>, <code>f</code></td></tr><tr><td>Run the build</td><td><code>r</code>, <code>b</code></td></tr><tr><td>Review and run a pipeline</td><td><code>p</code>, <code>↓</code> to the pipeline, <code>Enter</code>; see <a href="/guide/pipeline-terminal.html">Pipelines in the terminal</a></td></tr><tr><td>Validate a config</td><td><code>c</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>v</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="which-keys-work-on-every-screen" tabindex="-1">Which keys work on every screen? <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-keys-work-on-every-screen" aria-label="Permalink to “Which keys work on every screen?”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Go back, or cancel the operation in flight</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+C</code></td><td>Quit</td></tr><tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>Help overlay. Any key closes it</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift+L</code></td><td>Toggle the log viewer</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift+Q</code></td><td>Open the SQL terminal</td></tr><tr><td><code>D</code></td><td>Toggle dry-run mode. The status bar shows <code>DRY</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>F</code></td><td>Toggle force mode. The status bar shows <code>FORCE</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>D</code> applies to the run screens, pipeline review, <code>change ff</code>, and the database wipe, teardown, and transfer screens. In pipeline review it selects the next invocation&#39;s dry-run mode; a started invocation keeps its captured mode. <code>F</code> applies to the file run screens and <code>change ff</code>, but has no pipeline effect and cannot bypass pipeline policy or single-run protection.</p><p>While a text field takes input (a form field in edit mode, a search box, the SQL editor), every key above except <code>Ctrl+C</code> types its character instead. <code>?</code>, <code>D</code>, and <code>F</code> also stand down inside dialogs.</p><h3 id="lists" tabindex="-1">Lists <a class="header-anchor" href="#lists" aria-label="Permalink to “Lists”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>Move the cursor. Wraps at both ends, except on the changes list</td></tr><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Select</td></tr><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Back</td></tr><tr><td><code>1</code>-<code>9</code></td><td>Select by number, on lists that show numbers: Settings and the explorer</td></tr><tr><td><code>Space</code></td><td>Toggle an item, on multi-select lists. <code>Enter</code> then submits</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A list fits the window, so a taller terminal shows more rows. It keeps its cursor when you open an item and come back.</p><h3 id="forms" tabindex="-1">Forms <a class="header-anchor" href="#forms" aria-label="Permalink to “Forms”">​</a></h3><p>A form has two modes. <code>Enter</code> switches between them, so submitting is a place you navigate to:</p>',12)),(d(),s(n,null,{default:t(()=>[i(a,{id:"mermaid-332",class:"mermaid",graph:"stateDiagram-v2%0A%20%20%20%20%5B*%5D%20--%3E%20Browse%0A%20%20%20%20Browse%20--%3E%20Edit%3A%20Enter%20on%20a%20text%20or%20select%20field%0A%20%20%20%20Edit%20--%3E%20Browse%3A%20Enter%20commits%0A%20%20%20%20Edit%20--%3E%20Browse%3A%20Esc%20reverts%0A%20%20%20%20Browse%20--%3E%20Actions%3A%20Down%20past%20the%20last%20field%0A%20%20%20%20Actions%20--%3E%20Browse%3A%20Down%20wraps%20to%20the%20first%20field%0A%20%20%20%20Actions%20--%3E%20%5B*%5D%3A%20Enter%20on%20the%20submit%20button%0A"})]),fallback:t(()=>[...e[1]||(e[1]=[r(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[5]||(e[5]=o(`<table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Key</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Browse</td><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code>, <code>Tab</code>, <code>Shift+Tab</code></td><td>Move between fields, wrapping through the action row</td></tr><tr><td>Browse</td><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Open the field for editing</td></tr><tr><td>Browse</td><td><code>x</code></td><td>On a password field with a value, empty it. Opening a password field starts from its current value, so this replaces it without backspacing</td></tr><tr><td>Browse</td><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Cancel the form</td></tr><tr><td>Edit</td><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Commit and return to browse</td></tr><tr><td>Edit</td><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Restore the value the field held when edit opened</td></tr><tr><td>Edit</td><td><code>Tab</code>, <code>Shift+Tab</code></td><td>Commit and move to the next or previous field</td></tr><tr><td>Actions</td><td><code>←</code> <code>→</code>, <code>Enter</code></td><td>Pick a button and activate it</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A checkbox toggles in place on <code>Enter</code> or <code>Space</code>. A select expands on <code>Enter</code>, moves with <code>↑</code> <code>↓</code>, and takes the highlighted option on a second <code>Enter</code>. A red <code>*</code> marks a required field. Most forms reject an empty required field and put the cursor on it with an error. Pipeline input forms instead require presence: explicitly saving an empty string is valid.</p><p>A value longer than the row ends in <code>…</code> instead of wrapping, and an open text field scrolls to keep the cursor in view. On the config edit form, an empty password keeps the stored one, as its placeholder says: clear the field with <code>x</code> and type the replacement.</p><h3 id="mouse" tabindex="-1">Mouse <a class="header-anchor" href="#mouse" aria-label="Permalink to “Mouse”">​</a></h3><p>On by default. In lists and result grids:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Click a row</td><td>Move the cursor to it</td></tr><tr><td>Double-click a row</td><td>Same as <code>Enter</code> on that row</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel</td><td>Move the cursor one row, or scroll a document</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In a form:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Click a field</td><td>Move the cursor to it. Another field being edited is committed first</td></tr><tr><td>Double-click a text, password, or select field</td><td>Open it for editing</td></tr><tr><td>Click a checkbox</td><td>Toggle it</td></tr><tr><td>Click <code>[ Submit ]</code> or <code>[ Cancel ]</code></td><td>Activate that button</td></tr><tr><td>Click an option in an open select</td><td>Highlight it. Double-click takes it</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel</td><td>Move between fields, or the highlight in an open select</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In a confirm dialog, clicking <code>Yes (y)</code> confirms and clicking <code>No (n)</code> cancels.</p><p>A <code>[key] Label</code> hint is a button: a footer hint such as <code>[a] Add</code> or <code>[Esc] Back</code>, a Quick Actions entry, a row of Available Actions, or a menu entry on More. A click presses that key, through the same path the keyboard takes, so whatever has focus answers it exactly as it would the key. A double click presses once. Hints that name no single key (<code>[↑↓] Scroll</code>, <code>[any] Back</code>) and sentences that only mention a key (&quot;Press [a] to add one&quot;) are plain text; the Home prompts &quot;Press [c] to ...&quot; are the exception and are buttons.</p><p>A hint does nothing while a confirmation holds the screen, since the key would answer the confirmation. It never types its letter into a text field that is taking input; <code>Esc</code> and <code>Enter</code> hints still work there.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Also clickable</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Earlier breadcrumb crumbs</td><td>Go back to that screen. Inactive while an overlay is open, a confirmation holds the screen, or an operation is running</td></tr><tr><td>The idle <code>/ Filter...</code> row of a searchable list</td><td>Enter search mode</td></tr><tr><td>Rows of the file picker</td><td>Click moves the cursor and enters select mode; double-click toggles the checkbox</td></tr><tr><td>Rows of the log viewer, SQL history and clear lists, known users, result grids</td><td>Click moves the cursor; double-click does what <code>Enter</code> does</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Moving the pointer over anything a click would act on shades it, so you can see what is clickable. The shade is a step off your terminal&#39;s own background (lighter on a dark theme, darker on a light one), asked for when <code>noorm ui</code> starts, and the text keeps its color. A terminal that does not report its background gets ANSI bright black instead. The cursor row keeps its own marker and is not shaded. Nothing shades, or answers a click, in a list that does not hold focus.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title custom-block-title-default">TIP</p><p>Mouse tracking takes click-drag text selection away from the terminal. Hold Option in macOS Terminal and iTerm2, or Shift in most others.</p><p>Hover makes the terminal send a report for every cell the pointer crosses, which is noticeable over a slow SSH link. To turn the mouse off, set <code>ui.mouse</code> in <code>.noorm/settings.yml</code>; <code>?</code> prints the same hint. If a crashed session leaves the terminal printing characters as the pointer moves, run <code>reset</code>.</p><div class="language-yaml"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">yaml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># .noorm/settings.yml</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">ui</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">    mouse</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">false</span></span></code></pre></div></div><h2 id="what-does-esc-do-while-the-database-is-busy" tabindex="-1">What does Esc do while the database is busy? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-esc-do-while-the-database-is-busy" aria-label="Permalink to “What does Esc do while the database is busy?”">​</a></h2><p>A screen waiting on the database says so and names the key:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Testing connection...  [Esc] Cancel</span></span></code></pre></div><p>That covers testing a config (add and edit), the force-release lock check, and the SQL terminal connecting or running a query. <code>Esc</code> returns the screen to a usable state and saves nothing. The client always stops waiting. Whether the server stops depends on the dialect:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dialect</th><th>Effect on the server</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>pg_cancel_backend</code> asks it to stop the statement</td></tr><tr><td>MySQL</td><td><code>KILL QUERY</code> stops the statement</td></tr><tr><td>SQL Server, SQLite</td><td>Nothing. The statement finishes. The message reads <code>Stopped waiting. The query may still be running on the server.</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A connect that answers after you cancelled is closed, not adopted. A connection attempt also ends on its own after 15 seconds. Raise <code>connection.connectTimeoutMs</code> for a link that is slow but working. <a href="/guide/database/terminal.html">The SQL terminal guide</a> has the full table.</p><p>Pipeline stop uses a different contract: <code>Esc</code> requests that no more steps start, then waits for running SQL, transaction cleanup, and bookkeeping. It does not send the server-cancel requests in the table above. See <a href="/guide/pipeline-terminal.html#stop-and-quit-without-assuming-rollback">pipeline stop and shutdown</a>.</p><h3 id="long-running-files" tabindex="-1">Long-Running Files <a class="header-anchor" href="#long-running-files" aria-label="Permalink to “Long-Running Files”">​</a></h3><p>A file that runs past 10 seconds gets a status block under its name, refreshed every 10 seconds. It appears on the run screens (Build, Exec, Dir, File) and the change screens (Run, Revert, Fast Forward, Next, Rewind):</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Current:  06_items.sql</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Running for 17m05s  pid 4412</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>CREATE INDEX on items · building index: loading tuples · 4210/9800 (42%)  2 parallel workers</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Waiting on pid 4411 (CREATE INDEX items_embedding ..., 17m04s) · Lock: transactionid</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Appears when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Running for … pid …</code></td><td>Always. SQLite shows elapsed time only</td></tr><tr><td>Operation progress</td><td>The server reports it: index builds, <code>VACUUM</code>, <code>CLUSTER</code>, <code>COPY</code>, <code>ANALYZE</code> on PostgreSQL; <code>percent_complete</code> on SQL Server; stage counters on MySQL</td></tr><tr><td><code>Waiting on pid …</code></td><td>The file waits on another session&#39;s lock</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On Run Build and Run Exec, press <code>Esc</code> once for a warning and again within 2 seconds to cancel. No further file starts. PostgreSQL and MySQL cancel the running statement. SQL Server and SQLite let it finish, and the screen says <code>Stopping after the current file finishes.</code> Leaving the screen does not cancel the run.</p><h2 id="what-does-each-screen-do" tabindex="-1">What does each screen do? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-each-screen-do" aria-label="Permalink to “What does each screen do?”">​</a></h2><h3 id="config-list" tabindex="-1">Config List <a class="header-anchor" href="#config-list" aria-label="Permalink to “Config List”">​</a></h3><p><img src="`+m+'" alt="The configuration list" loading="lazy"></p><p>A config shows <code>●</code> when active and <code>○</code> when not, with <code>[user:&lt;role&gt; agent:&lt;role|off&gt;]</code> when its access differs from the default (<code>admin</code> for you, <code>viewer</code> for the agent) and <code>[test]</code> on a test config.</p><p>A server config also shows its TLS state, read from the connection the TUI opens for it:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[tls]</code></td><td>SSL is on and the server certificate is validated</td></tr><tr><td><code>[tls unverified]</code></td><td>SSL is on and <code>ssl.rejectUnauthorized</code> is <code>false</code>: the connection is encrypted and the certificate is not validated</td></tr><tr><td>none</td><td>SSL is off, the config is SQLite, the config names an access role the project does not define, or the stored config and its stage do not combine into a valid config</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The TUI connects with the stored config over the <code>defaults</code> of the <a href="/guide/environments/stages.html#where-do-defaults-apply">stage</a> with its name, so a config with no <code>ssl</code> of its own on a stage that sets <code>ssl</code> shows <code>[tls]</code>. The TUI applies no config variable (<code>NOORM_CONNECTION_*</code>, <code>NOORM_ACCESS_*</code>) and no flag to a config. Settings variables still apply: <code>NOORM_STAGES_&lt;stage&gt;_DEFAULTS_*</code> changes the stage defaults the TUI merges, as <code>NOORM_PATHS_*</code> changes its paths.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>The TUI shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The stored config and its stage do not combine into a valid config, for example a <code>pool.min</code> above the default <code>pool.max</code></td><td>Home with the validation message and no active config. The config stays in this list, where <code>e</code> edits it</td></tr><tr><td>A certificate file cannot be read</td><td>The connection error with its message, such as <code>Cannot read ssl.caFile &#39;./certs/corp-ca.pem&#39;: ENOENT</code>. Home and the database list do not report the database as missing, whatever the path contains</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Make the highlighted config active. On the config that is already active, open its edit form</td></tr><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>Add</td></tr><tr><td><code>e</code></td><td>Edit</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>Delete</td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>Copy</td></tr><tr><td><code>k</code></td><td>Secrets of the highlighted config</td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>More: <code>x</code> export, <code>i</code> import, <code>v</code> validate</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Adding a config is interactive only. <code>noorm config add</code> points here, and <code>noorm config import &lt;file.json&gt;</code> is the headless route.</p><p><img src="'+f+'" alt="The add-config form" loading="lazy"></p><p>Both forms also carry these SSL fields. The edit form of a SQLite config shows none of them.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Sets</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Use SSL</td><td><code>ssl</code></td><td>Checked turns SSL on. Unchecking it stores <code>ssl: false</code>, which also turns off an <code>ssl</code> the stage supplies</td></tr><tr><td>Verify Certificate</td><td><code>ssl.rejectUnauthorized</code></td><td>Unchecked with Use SSL checked, it stores <code>ssl.rejectUnauthorized: false</code>: the connection stays encrypted and the server certificate is not validated. Checking it again removes the <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td>CA File</td><td><code>ssl.caFile</code></td><td>Path to the CA certificate. A path replaces inline PEM of the same field. Emptied, it removes the stored path</td></tr><tr><td>Client Cert File</td><td><code>ssl.certFile</code></td><td>Path to the client certificate</td></tr><tr><td>Client Key File</td><td><code>ssl.keyFile</code></td><td>Path to the client key</td></tr><tr><td>TLS Server Name</td><td><code>tlsServerName</code></td><td>Hostname the server certificate is issued for</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The edit form opens on the state in effect, which is the stored config over its stage. The add form shows no stage state, whatever name you type: Use SSL starts unchecked, Verify Certificate starts checked, and no field shows a stage placeholder.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>On the edit form</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The stored config or the stage turns SSL on</td><td>Use SSL checked</td></tr><tr><td>The <code>ssl</code> in effect has <code>rejectUnauthorized: false</code></td><td>Verify Certificate unchecked</td></tr><tr><td>Only the stage supplies a certificate file</td><td>An empty field with the placeholder <code>(stage: &lt;path&gt;)</code></td></tr><tr><td>The stored value is inline PEM</td><td>An empty field with the placeholder <code>(inline certificate stored)</code>. The PEM stays while the field is empty</td></tr><tr><td>The stored config has <code>ssl: false</code></td><td>Use SSL unchecked and no stage placeholder. Checking it stores <code>ssl: true</code>, and the stage&#39;s certificate files apply again</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A save that changes none of Use SSL, Verify Certificate and the three file fields leaves the stored <code>ssl</code> as it was, and an absent <code>ssl</code> stays absent. The stage keeps supplying its value, and the saved config holds no copy of it. An unchanged TLS Server Name is left as stored in the same way. Typed paths and the server name are trimmed. The edit form keeps every connection field it does not show, such as <code>pool</code> and timeouts, except a SQLite <code>filename</code>, which the Database field replaces.</p><p>Submit judges the connection the save would produce over the stage&#39;s <code>defaults</code>, on both forms. The message shows on the form and nothing is saved.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Submit refuses</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A certificate path you enter or change</td><td>SSL would be off in the result</td></tr><tr><td>A TLS Server Name on PostgreSQL or MySQL</td><td>SSL would be off in the result, or Verify Certificate is unchecked</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A stage applies by config name, so renaming a config on the edit form can move it to another stage, or to none. The save then stores the TLS state the form shows (<code>ssl: true</code>, <code>ssl: false</code>, or <code>rejectUnauthorized: false</code>) when the new name would give a different one. The old stage&#39;s certificate paths stay with the stage: enter them on the config if the renamed config needs them.</p><p>A TLS Server Name passes when the stage supplies <code>ssl</code> and you leave Use SSL untouched. MSSQL allows the name in every case. Unchecking Use SSL over stored paths saves <code>ssl: false</code> and drops the paths. The connection test also runs over the stage&#39;s <code>defaults</code>, and a path that cannot be read shows its error there. <a href="/guide/environments/configs.html#how-do-i-connect-to-a-server-with-a-private-ca">Configs</a> covers the path rules.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action on the add and edit forms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Enter</code> on the submit button</td><td>Test the server connection, then save</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+S</code></td><td>Save without testing the connection</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>Ctrl+S</code> works while the cursor moves between fields. While a field is in edit mode it does nothing and types nothing; press <code>Enter</code> or <code>Tab</code> to leave edit mode first. It runs the same field validation as the submit button. The hint row shows it as <code>ctrl+s save without verifying</code>. A failed test prints its error under the hint row, then <code>Press Ctrl+S to save without verifying.</code> on a row of its own, which is hidden while a field is in edit mode. Use it for a database that is unreachable now, such as one behind a tunnel that is down.</p><p>Saving a config needs an identity, because the private key encrypts <code>.noorm/state/state.enc</code>. When none exists, Add (<code>a</code>) and Import (<code>i</code> under More) show identity setup first:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Saving a config needs an identity. Set it up once to continue.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Name and email are filled in from <code>git config user.name</code> and <code>git config user.email</code>; the name falls back to the OS username. Machine is filled in from the hostname. Submitting creates the identity and continues to the form you opened. <code>Esc</code> goes back and creates nothing.</p><p>Setup never replaces an existing <code>~/.noorm/identity.key</code>. With a key and no <code>identity.pub</code>, it derives the public key from the private one; with a key and <code>identity.json</code> already there, it asks nothing. When the key file exists but cannot be loaded, the screen shows the reason instead of the form, for example <code>Insecure permissions on private key file (...). Fix with: chmod 600 ...</code>.</p><p>Export (<code>x</code> under More) writes <code>&lt;name&gt;.noorm.enc</code>, encrypted for one recipient, and import (<code>i</code>) reads it. <a href="/dev/config-sharing.html">Config sharing</a> has the file format.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Export carries</td><td>Every stored connection field that is not a credential, including <code>dialect</code>, <code>host</code>, <code>port</code>, <code>database</code>, <code>tlsServerName</code>, <code>pool</code>, the timeouts, and in <code>ssl</code> the inline <code>ca</code>, <code>rejectUnauthorized</code> and the three file paths. Stage values are not added</td></tr><tr><td>Export withholds</td><td><code>user</code>, <code>password</code>, and the inline <code>ssl.key</code> and <code>ssl.cert</code>. The recipient supplies their own</td></tr><tr><td>Import preview</td><td>Sender and secrets count, dialect, database and host, then SSL on or off, certificate verification, the TLS server name and each certificate file path. <code>Roles: user &lt;role&gt;, agent &lt;role|off&gt;</code> shows the roles the import saves. A file that sets <code>isTest</code> adds <code>Test database: every role gets at least admin&#39;s permissions</code></td></tr><tr><td>Import saves</td><td>The connection fields the config schema accepts, with the user and password typed on the import form</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A certificate file path travels as text. The preview says <code>A certificate file path must exist on this machine.</code>, and import does not check for the file.</p><p><code>User Role</code> covers a human on the CLI, TUI, or SDK. <code>Agent Role</code> covers an AI agent over MCP and the CLI. They are independent. <a href="/guide/environments/configs.html#access-roles">Configs</a> lists what each role permits.</p><h3 id="changes-list" tabindex="-1">Changes List <a class="header-anchor" href="#changes-list" aria-label="Permalink to “Changes List”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+u+'" alt="The changes list" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>✓</code></td><td>Applied</td></tr><tr><td><code>○</code></td><td>Pending</td></tr><tr><td><code>✗</code></td><td>Failed</td></tr><tr><td><code>↩</code></td><td>Reverted</td></tr><tr><td><code>!</code></td><td>Orphaned: recorded in the database, folder gone from disk</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Run a pending, failed, or reverted change. Revert an applied one</td></tr><tr><td><code>a</code></td><td>Add</td></tr><tr><td><code>e</code></td><td>Edit. Not for an orphaned change</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>Delete</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Run. Pending, failed, or reverted only</td></tr><tr><td><code>v</code></td><td>Revert. Applied only</td></tr><tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>Next: apply the next N pending changes, one by default</td></tr><tr><td><code>f</code></td><td>Fast-forward all pending changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>w</code></td><td>Rewind</td></tr><tr><td><code>h</code></td><td>History</td></tr></tbody></table><p>With no changes, the list reads <code>No changes found. Press [a] to create one.</code></p><p><img src="'+b+'" alt="Change history" loading="lazy"></p><p>History lists what ran, when, and under which identity. <code>Enter</code> shows the files, and <code>e</code> shows the full error of a failed change.</p><h3 id="run-menu" tabindex="-1">Run Menu <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-menu" aria-label="Permalink to “Run Menu”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+y+'" alt="The run menu" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>b</code> or <code>1</code> Build, <code>e</code> or <code>2</code> Exec, <code>f</code> or <code>3</code> File, <code>d</code> or <code>4</code> Dir, <code>i</code> or <code>5</code> Inspect.</p><p>A file that already ran with the same content opens <strong>File Previously Run</strong> and asks <code>Run this file again?</code>. A directory with such files opens <strong>Files Previously Run</strong>.</p><h3 id="pipelines" tabindex="-1">Pipelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#pipelines" aria-label="Permalink to “Pipelines”">​</a></h3><p>Pipeline review and history are separate screens; recorded runs remain reachable without opening a definition:</p>',69)),(d(),s(n,null,{default:t(()=>[i(a,{id:"mermaid-1348",class:"mermaid",graph:"flowchart%20TD%0A%20%20%20%20Home%5B%22Home%22%5D%20--%3E%7Cp%7C%20Pipelines%5B%22Pipeline%20list%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Pipelines%20--%3E%7CEnter%7C%20Review%5B%22Pipeline%20review%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Pipelines%20--%3E%7Ch%7C%20History%5B%22Pipeline%20history%22%5D%0A%20%20%20%20Review%20--%3E%7Ch%7C%20History%0A%20%20%20%20History%20--%3E%7CEnter%7C%20Detail%5B%22Run%20detail%22%5D%0A"})]),fallback:t(()=>[...e[2]||(e[2]=[r(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[6]||(e[6]=o('<p><code>p</code> from Home opens code-owned pipeline definitions. <code>Enter</code> reviews a definition, <code>s</code> switches inputs and steps, and <code>r</code> opens native run or dry-run confirmation against the captured active config. Each input uses a separate form with explicit <strong>Save</strong> and <strong>Cancel</strong>; <code>x</code> in review removes its override.</p><p><code>h</code> opens config-scoped history by slug, and <code>i</code> on the pipeline list opens run-ID lookup, even without a local definition. Results distinguish execution status from transaction outcome and show cleanup warnings separately. The screens show elapsed time rather than live SQL or step progress.</p><p>See <a href="/guide/pipeline-terminal.html">Pipelines in the terminal</a> for typed input presence, review and result keys, protected confirmation, bounded history, and stop/shutdown behavior.</p><h3 id="database-menu" tabindex="-1">Database Menu <a class="header-anchor" href="#database-menu" aria-label="Permalink to “Database Menu”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+w+'" alt="The database menu" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th><th>Needs a connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>Create the database. Shows a toast if it is already initialized</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>d</code></td><td>Destroy</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>x</code></td><td>Explore</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>w</code></td><td>Wipe: truncate</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>Teardown</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Transfer</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The SQL terminal is not on this menu. Open it with <code>Shift+Q</code>.</p><h3 id="schema-explorer" tabindex="-1">Schema Explorer <a class="header-anchor" href="#schema-explorer" aria-label="Permalink to “Schema Explorer”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+k+'" alt="The explorer overview, with object counts by category" loading="lazy"></p><p>Each category opens from the overview by its letter or its number. A category with no objects does nothing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Keys</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tables</td><td><code>t</code>, <code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Views</td><td><code>v</code>, <code>2</code></td></tr><tr><td>Procedures</td><td><code>p</code>, <code>3</code></td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td><code>f</code>, <code>4</code></td></tr><tr><td>Types</td><td><code>y</code>, <code>5</code></td></tr><tr><td>Indexes</td><td><code>i</code>, <code>6</code></td></tr><tr><td>Foreign Keys</td><td><code>k</code>, <code>7</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><img src="'+v+'" alt="The table list" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>Enter</code> on an object opens its detail. Indexes and foreign keys have none.</p><p><img src="'+S+'" alt="Table detail: columns, indexes, and foreign keys" loading="lazy"></p><p>A detail longer than the window scrolls. The footer lists only the keys the screen answers to.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>Scroll one line</td></tr><tr><td><code>Ctrl+U</code>, <code>Ctrl+D</code></td><td>Half a page</td></tr><tr><td><code>PageUp</code>, <code>PageDown</code></td><td>A full page. On macOS the footer shows <code>fn ↑↓</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Home</code>, <code>End</code></td><td>Top, bottom</td></tr><tr><td><code>v</code></td><td>Full text: the same rows with nothing truncated</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Read rows. Tables only</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="reading-rows" tabindex="-1">Reading Rows <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-rows" aria-label="Permalink to “Reading Rows”">​</a></h3><p><code>r</code> on a table detail reads its rows without leaving the explorer for the SQL terminal.</p><p><img src="'+T+'" alt="Peeking at both ends of a table, then opening one row as a document" loading="lazy"></p><p>The peek reads both ends of the table, so the last rows by primary key are as reachable as the first:</p><p><img src="'+E+'" alt="First and last rows, side by side" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>Move the cursor within a set</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tab</code></td><td>Swap between the first set and the last</td></tr><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Open the highlighted row as a document</td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>Filter. <code>Tab</code> cycles the column, <code>Enter</code> applies, <code>Esc</code> clears</td></tr><tr><td><code>s</code></td><td>Sort. <code>←</code> <code>→</code> pick the column, <code>a</code> ascending, <code>d</code> descending</td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code></td><td>Clear filter and sort</td></tr><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Close the peek</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The table has no primary key</td><td>Only the first rows appear: <code>No primary key, so there is no last set — rows are in storage order.</code></td></tr><tr><td>Both ends meet</td><td>One table, headed <code>All N rows</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The grid fits as many whole columns as the terminal holds and marks the rest <code>… N more columns</code>. <code>Enter</code> on a row shows them all:</p><p><img src="'+x+'" alt="One row as a YAML document" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>←</code> <code>→</code></td><td>Previous row, next row, in the order the table showed</td></tr><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>Scroll a document taller than the window</td></tr><tr><td><code>f</code></td><td>Swap YAML and JSON. The choice holds for the session</td></tr><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Back to the peek, on the row you were reading</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="sql-terminal" tabindex="-1">SQL Terminal <a class="header-anchor" href="#sql-terminal" aria-label="Permalink to “SQL Terminal”">​</a></h3><p><code>Shift+Q</code> opens the SQL terminal against the active config from any screen.</p><p><img src="'+A+'" alt="The SQL terminal" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Run the query</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift+Enter</code></td><td>New line</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift+Tab</code></td><td>Toggle edit mode, where <code>Enter</code> inserts a new line</td></tr><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>Walk query history, when the box is empty or already browsing</td></tr><tr><td><code>h</code></td><td>Open the history list, when the box is empty</td></tr><tr><td><code>Tab</code></td><td>Switch between the query and the results, once there are rows</td></tr><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>In results: back to the query. With text in the box: clear it. Empty: go back. While a query runs: cancel it</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In the results grid, <code>/</code>, <code>s</code>, and <code>c</code> filter, sort, and clear as in the row peek, and <code>Enter</code> opens a row as a document. A result wider than the terminal keeps whole columns and marks the remainder <code>… N more columns</code>.</p><p>The history list takes <code>↑</code> <code>↓</code>, <code>Enter</code> to view a result, <code>r</code> to re-run a query, and <code>c</code> to clear.</p><h3 id="log-viewer" tabindex="-1">Log Viewer <a class="header-anchor" href="#log-viewer" aria-label="Permalink to “Log Viewer”">​</a></h3><p><code>Shift+L</code> toggles an overlay that tails events as they happen, newest first.</p><p><img src="'+C+'" alt="The log viewer overlay" loading="lazy"></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>Search. <code>Enter</code> leaves search, <code>Esc</code> clears it</td></tr><tr><td><code>g</code>, <code>G</code></td><td>Newest, oldest</td></tr><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code>, <code>PageUp</code>, <code>PageDown</code></td><td>Move</td></tr><tr><td><code>Space</code></td><td>Pause or resume the tail</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code></td><td>Refresh</td></tr><tr><td><code>Enter</code></td><td>Open one entry in full. <code>Esc</code> returns to the list</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift+L</code>, <code>Esc</code></td><td>Close</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="more" tabindex="-1">More <a class="header-anchor" href="#more" aria-label="Permalink to “More”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+_+'" alt="The More menu" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>+</code> from Home opens Settings (<code>s</code>), Vault (<code>v</code>), Identity (<code>i</code>), and Lock (<code>l</code>). Each also has its direct key on Home.</p><h3 id="settings" tabindex="-1">Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings" aria-label="Permalink to “Settings”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+L+'" alt="The settings screen" loading="lazy"></p><p>Eight groups: Build, Paths, Stages, Rules, Strict, Logging, Secrets, and Access Roles. <code>Enter</code> or a number opens one, and edits write to <code>.noorm/settings.yml</code>. <code>i</code> creates that file when the project has none. See <a href="/guide/sql-files/organization.html">SQL file organization</a> and <a href="/guide/environments/configs.html">Configs</a>.</p><h3 id="identity" tabindex="-1">Identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#identity" aria-label="Permalink to “Identity”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+P+'" alt="The identity screen" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>e</code> edit, <code>x</code> export your public key, <code>r</code> regenerate, <code>u</code> known users. Regenerating makes existing state on the machine unreadable, so it first copies the identity files to <code>&lt;file&gt;.bak-&lt;timestamp&gt;</code> in <code>~/.noorm/</code> and shows the paths; see the <a href="/cli/identity.html">warning on <code>identity init</code></a>. The identity lives in <code>~/.noorm/</code> and names who ran each change in the <a href="/guide/changes/history.html">history</a>. The CLI equivalent is <a href="/cli/identity.html"><code>noorm identity</code></a>.</p><h3 id="vault" tabindex="-1">Vault <a class="header-anchor" href="#vault" aria-label="Permalink to “Vault”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+R+'" alt="The vault screen before initialization" loading="lazy"></p><p>The vault holds team-shared encrypted secrets in the database. It starts uninitialized: <code>i</code> creates it. After that, <code>a</code> adds a secret, <code>Enter</code> edits the highlighted one, and <code>p</code> propagates access to teammates. See <a href="/guide/environments/vault.html">Vault</a>.</p><h3 id="secrets" tabindex="-1">Secrets <a class="header-anchor" href="#secrets" aria-label="Permalink to “Secrets”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+I+'" alt="The secrets screen for a config" loading="lazy"></p><p><code>k</code> on the config list opens the highlighted config&#39;s secrets: <code>a</code> add, <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> edit, <code>d</code> delete. See <a href="/guide/environments/secrets.html">Secrets</a>.</p><h3 id="lock" tabindex="-1">Lock <a class="header-anchor" href="#lock" aria-label="Permalink to “Lock”">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+D+'" alt="The lock screen" loading="lazy"></p><p>noorm locks the database around writes, so two people building one environment do not interleave. On the lock menu, <code>s</code> or <code>1</code> shows status, <code>a</code> or <code>2</code> acquires, <code>r</code> or <code>3</code> releases, and <code>f</code> or <code>4</code> force-breaks a stale lock. The status screen also has <code>x</code> to extend. See <a href="/dev/lock.html">Locking</a>.</p><p>Acquire and release need the <code>lock:acquire</code> permission, and force needs <code>lock:force</code>. A role the permission denies sees the policy refusal, and the screen opens no connection. Where the role&#39;s cell is <code>confirm</code>, the screen asks you to type <code>yes-&lt;config&gt;</code> before it acts.</p><h3 id="update" tabindex="-1">Update <a class="header-anchor" href="#update" aria-label="Permalink to “Update”">​</a></h3><p><code>u</code> from Home checks GitHub releases for a newer noorm. <code>i</code> installs it, <code>r</code> checks again, and <code>Esc</code> goes back.</p><p>A binary install reports its download as <code>42.0 / 73.0 MB (57%)</code> with a progress bar. Without a <code>Content-Length</code> it shows <code>9.0 MB received</code> and no bar. A stalled download resumes from the bytes on disk and shows <code>download stalled — no data for 30s — resuming (attempt N/5)...</code>. An npm install runs the package manager and shows none of this.</p><h2 id="what-does-a-role-that-cannot-write-see" tabindex="-1">What does a role that cannot write see? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-a-role-that-cannot-write-see" aria-label="Permalink to “What does a role that cannot write see?”">​</a></h2><p>Opening a screen as a role that can write creates noorm&#39;s tracking tables, or migrates older ones. A role denied every command that records to them, such as <code>viewer</code>, creates and migrates nothing. Activating a config follows the same rule, and registers no identity in the database for such a role. Its screens show:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Screen</th><th>On a database with no tracking tables</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Home</td><td>Connected, every change on disk counted as pending, lock free, no recent activity</td></tr><tr><td>Changes, change history</td><td>Every change pending, no history</td></tr><tr><td>Lock status, lock list</td><td>Unlocked</td></tr><tr><td>Lock acquire, release, force</td><td>The policy refusal. No connection is opened</td></tr><tr><td>Pipeline history, run lookup</td><td>No runs</td></tr><tr><td>Vault</td><td>The policy refusal, on the list, add and delete screens. No connection is opened</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When the tables need a migration, such a role sees the error the CLI prints: <code>tracking tables are at schema version &lt;n&gt; and this version needs &lt;m&gt;. The role on config &quot;&lt;name&gt;&quot; cannot migrate them: a role that can write must run a command once first.</code></p><h2 id="next" tabindex="-1">Next <a class="header-anchor" href="#next" aria-label="Permalink to “Next”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/headless.html">CLI reference</a>: the non-interactive surface</li><li><a href="/guide/database/terminal.html">The SQL terminal guide</a>: history, result browsing, per-dialect cancel</li><li><a href="/guide/sql-files/execution.html">Execution</a>: what the run screens do with each file</li><li><a href="/guide/pipeline-terminal.html">Pipelines in the terminal</a>: review, typed inputs, results, and scoped history</li><li><a href="/guide/environments/configs.html">Configs</a>: access roles</li></ul>',64))])}const z=c(N,[["render",M]]);export{q as __pageData,z as default};
